cludes extra funding for  local authorities to do this testing  up to a figure of 14 pounds per test  done so that's quite significant extra  funding that's available across the uk  for councils that want to make this  work on the model of liverpool and i'll  ask  a general messenger to set out more  details of how we can make it work  because this community testing approach  is so important to  finding the cases that we haven't yet  found  and so reducing the the number of  infections  which obviously is critical for  everybody  so first the um we're trying to move  away from the term mass testing i think  this  is about community testing it's about  tailoring it to the needs as i said of  the  of of the local area and that might be  geographically specific or it might be  ethnic community specific um and i  think the secret the secretary state's  right when he says the prospectus which  will  be public from now will set out a great  deal  of support and a great deal of  signposting for what is  on offer to these local authorities  and one could easily see an arrangement  where we're setting up some small  local targeted community testing sites  but equally when a community wants to  target broader swathes of the population  something larger something more sort of  ubiquitous  tackling larger swathes of of the  population it really depends on where  the need is  and will be driven by the local  leadership and the local directors  of uh of public health it it's important  to talk about the workforce here i think  because as you  uh rightly point out liverpool was  delivered primarily by  a military workforce and that  and i'm not speaking uh as part of the  ministry of defence i work for  the health secretary on the community  testing program but i can say with  confidence that cannot be  replicated around the country and  therefore  the military support along with all  other types of central support has to be  targeted where it's needed most and  where it can have  the greatest effect so i have absolutely  no doubt  that the military will continue to play  a really important part in the community  testing program but the baseline  assumption in terms of workforce  generation  is that that will be delivered locally  with considerable support  from the centre and from what i've seen  of a number of local communities there's  great ingenuity already underway  in delivering that workforce they're  accessing the volunteer force they're  working very  innovatively with uh the private sector  and yes of course they've got good  liaison with  local leadership in the the military  so that is our starting assumption as i  say i have no doubt that the military  will play a prominent role but the idea  that it can be a wholesale offer to all  local authorities is simply  undeliverable given uh  what the military uh are doing already  thanks very much andy bell from uh  channel five andy  all right secretary of state thank you  very much  um realistically is there any chance of  any part of england being able to move  down a tier  this side of christmas there's meant to  be a review in a couple of weeks time  isn't it much more likely that anyway  we'll have to wait until well into  january for any chance of moving down  the tier because  we'll have to work out what the effect  of the christmas  relaxation has been uh not necessarily  no  we will we'll look at the data on the  16th  of december and we'll announce the  results of that  review on the 17th of december of course  we've got to take into account that  christmas is  coming up but nevertheless with the case  rates coming down as they are  as i said coming down by almost a third  in the last  week then we will be absolutely  looking at each area and um  seeing what is the right tier for that  area  you know i want to see areas that have  pulled together followed the rules got  the case rate down  like liverpool has over the last month  which has led to it having going into  tier two  i want to see that happening much more  broadly and there are some areas of the  country that i'm  i'm worried about there are some parts  of the country  small parts of the country where the  numbers are still going up so we do have  to be careful and we have to be cautious  but the first review will be on the 16th  of december  we'll look at the whole of england at  that point  and then we'll continue to review  regularly after that  thanks very much uh next question is  from uh jim pickard at the ft  jim secretary of state um  you say today that there's nearly 16 000  people in hospital with  covet can you say what proportion of  those are in hospital because of covid  as opposed to people who are in hospital  for other underlying reasons such as  cancer or they've broken a leg and they  happen to have covered  and that's been picked up and also  nadine zahari the new vaccine minister  has suggested that  people who refuse to get vaccinated  could be not allowed to visit  restaurants bars cinemas and so on  is that kind of stick approach something  that you agree with  uh thanks very much well i'll ask steve  to answer the first one and then i'll  answer the second question  so the vast majority of people when we  have a pandemic and the infection rates  are high  are in because of the direct effects of  covid  we do see people in uh hospital uh with  covid  um when infection rates are high who  have not been admitted because of  covered  so it's a secondary state um highlighted  um  about a third of people have covered  without any symptoms  so we have for some time in the nhs been  testing everybody who gets admitted for  covert  to pick up whether they have it or not  and of course as infection rates  increase in the community then the  chance of picking up asymptomatic  individuals as they come into hospital  uh increases so we certainly do see  cases of individuals who've come into  hospital without covid who then test  positive  and that's why we've got very detailed  infection prevention and control  measures in  we not only test people when they arrive  in hospital we test people after they've  been in  hospital for a few days uh because it is  also possible that when you arrive  uh you may test negative so we're very  uh  careful to ensure that we're testing  people uh on a regular basis in their in  their first week in hospital  um because because that keeps patients  safe it keeps uh staff safe  uh but the important thing of course as  i always say is to get infection rates  down  because the lower the rate in the  community  the less the chance is that you will be  admitted into hospital for some other  condition  and turn out to test positive for covid  and on  on vaccines look our overall approach on  the vaccines is firstly that we do not  plan to mandate  the vaccine we think that by  encouraging the uptake of the vaccine we  will get a  very high proportion of people in this  country and to  um to take up the vaccine because of  course it is  it protects you but it also helps to  protect your loved ones and your  community  on the question of what happens once  you've had the vaccine  now of course in the first instance we  do not yet  have reliable data on whether a vaccine  not only protects you  because which we we do have a high  degree of confidence  uh subject to the mhra that i was  talking about in my comments um we do  have a high degree of confidence that  the  three vaccines so far protect the  person who's been vaccinated but the  impact on whether you transmit  the virus is not as clear  from the data so far and therefore in  the first instance our approach will be  to watch the indicators that we watch  all of the time the case rate the case  rate amongst the over 60s  and the positivity rate and um  to see the impact of that on the of the  vaccine and the vaccine program  on the uh on  on the pandemic now having said that for  a long time now we've been looking at  uh the the the questions that minister  zahawi was  talking about and the question of what's  the impact on the individual in terms of  what they can do and i've talked about  that at  press conferences like these months and  months ago  and so that's what minister zahawi was  referring to  and he is a great appointment and i  think will bring a  huge amount of support and uh  and scrutiny to the rollout of the  vaccine  and i think he's going to do a brilliant  job he's already been working on the  on the vaccine on the the business  department side of the fence  and i'm really delighted that he's  joined the department today  next question is from kate pickles at  the daily mail  thank you we have heard concerns from  the faculty of public health about the  enormous resources required for mass  testing  and the suggestion that it could detract  from the vaccine program  firstly i wondered how you'd respond to  that and how confident you are that the  two things can successfully run  alongside one another  secondly is the expansion of community  testing and admission that test and  trace hasn't worked  as well as you would have liked and how  can you envisage test and trace  changing as a result of this new program  thanks very much well i can reassure you  on both counts  the first thing is that the community  testing program  is a big expansion of what we can do  within nhs test and trace and it's only  because of the work of nhs test and  trace over the last  nine months the the work of the team  that has got these massive new testing  capabilities that allow us to make these  expansions  so that work has been the work of nhs  testing trace has been absolutely  critical in getting us to this point  when we can roll out  such uh such widespread use of the  of the tests for instance today to  university  students and then as general messenger  was saying  more broadly to the community working  with local directors of public health  um on the um on the first  part of the question the key point is  that testing  and vaccination need to run alongside  each other and this has already been  happening  for instance in the vaccine trials we  also tested  repeatedly some of the people's with  people within the trials  to find out if they picked up covert and  that's one of the ways that you can find  out if a vaccine can work  and especially because once you've taken  a vaccine  it still takes some time to  get immunity through the vaccine not  least because you have to have two doses  that it's critical that we have the  testing program running alongside the  vaccine program  and so they are designed to be symbiotic  i'll ask  general messenger to say a little bit  more about this but you know the last  thing i'll say on  it is this um the rollout that we can  announce today  of the community testing program is  a sign of the success of nhs test and  trace  in delivering the massive capability  that we now have  to be able to keep more people safe and  to find this  virus and to get it under control with  the  minimal negative impact on people's  lives that all of these things have  general messenger um well i i'm all of  uh all of three weeks in but uh  my experience thus far absolutely  supports what the secretary of state  says  the importance of alignment with what  test and trace have achieved so far  the vital nature of the alignment with  the vaccine  uh we we're absolutely we have to ensure  that those are  um synchronous and not competitive  i can tell you how supportive colleagues  have been in the vaccine task force and  the test and trace their experience  their skills  their knowledge has been vital as we've  uh as we've looked to build this  community  testing uh proposal so  to me that alignment is there but we're  absolutely certain that we cannot allow  uh each other to disrupt or deflect from  that the important business we're all  doing  thanks very much kate the final question  is from david hughes at the pa david  secretary of state tomorrow mps will  vote on these new tiers arrangements  the impact assessment the government has  published today makes clear that without  restrictions  the nhs could be overwhelmed given that  are your colleagues on the tory benches  who are thinking of voting against these  measures acting irresponsibly  and for general messenger 23 million  people will be going into tier 3 this  week  what sort of proportion of those people  would you expect your community testing  exercise to cover thanks very much  david well i would urge all mps right  across the house  to vote for the tiered system the tiered  system  is has a lower set of restrictions than  the national lockdown in  all three tiers but it's also more  targeted  so that the areas where the prevalence  is lower and the pressure on the nhs is  lower  have fewer restrictions which means that  people can  do more in terms of going about their  their their normal lives  and obviously has a less detrimental  impact on the  economy unfortunately though we do have  to have  the higher tier restrictions and in  particular tier three restrictions in  place  so that we can have confidence that we  can keep getting this  virus down and then keep it under  control  um right across the country  and that way it is the best way to avoid  a third lockdown and it is the most  proportionate way  to take the action that we need to keep  people safe  and to stop the nhs being overwhelmed so  i hope that we get  a very significant proportion of the uh  of mps  from all parties uh voting for the  proposals that we'll put before the  house  tomorrow i don't know professor pass if  you've got anything to add from the nhs  side  and then i'll ask general messenger to  answer the second question no no simply  to reiterate the importance as the nhs  goes into its busiest time of year to  ensure  that infection rates are low and falling  because as sure as nightfall as day that  is  two weeks later unfortunately  more hospital admissions uh more people  in hospital  uh more pressure on nhs services and of  course unfortunately more deaths  and increasingly as we understand  conditions such as long covered  uh more uh long-term health problems for  people so  the key as always is to get infection  rates down  and to ensure that they continue to stay  down  uh thanks very much uh general messages  um  david i don't know how many of those 23  million will be able to to reach because  planning is still  very much uh underway and as i said will  uh  the the nature of this will vary uh from  region to region and from area to area  uh we're alive to the the possibility  that we will not be able to do this all  at once  um but we assess that uh local  authorities are  at varying levels of of preparedness and  readiness uh  to to do this and therefore i can see  this as an offer  that will uh sequence over time uh and  into  january uh and beyond uh the priority  that we accord  uh the levels of support we give will be  based on the infection  rate how long those areas have been  under  harsh restrictions how mature their  plans are  how ready they are to deliver them and  that's the business of the next week or  so is to try and identify  some front runners and and to lean into  support of them so we can have a quick  early impact but as i say i can see this  being sequenced over time  thanks very much the the only thing i'd  add to that is that i'd urge  all those council leaders  director of public health and those in  the devolved administrations who want to  take up this offer that set out in the  prospectus  those within the tier three areas in  england and the da's  with whom we've been working to come  forward and come forward with their  proposals  often they know their communities best  and so we want to work  hand in glove with local directors of  public health with local authorities and  of course with  colleagues and mps who represent those  areas  to make sure that we can use this  community testing program  as effectively as possible to root out  this virus  and also then hopefully to free up  people to be able to do some of the  things that we love and  and that way we can better get through  this together  thanks very much indeed that ends  today's coronavirus briefing from  number 10 downing street  there we are matt hancock the health  secretary there um  quoting the research that he says shows  uh the lockdown that's just  ending has worked he said it had brought  down  cases by almost a third and that he said  was good news and means pressure on the  nhs had been  uh reduced and that the virus he said  was back under control  the r8 he said was back uh below one and  he said that was the success of  collective efforts  and it means some areas can have more  freedoms but  we cannot relax it is not the moment  to relax he said it is the moment to  stand firm  and he said the light of dawn is on the  horizon we'll talk to uh  john craig in just a moment about that  news conference just a little bit of  breaking news first and it is that the  british government has uh  decided not to order an immediate public  inquiry into  the north belfast solicitor pat for  newton's  shooting dead by loyalist paramilitaries  in collusion with the state back in 1989  that's according to the  northern ireland secretary brandon  lewis and the family  have been reacting to our correspondent  stephen murphy  about that decision no public inquiry or  no immediate public inquiry  and the family have told stephen murphy  that they've been told that the police  service of northern ireland the  ombudsman investigations must  uh run their course and uh john fanucan  has told uh  sky news that that was an insult and  less  than a sop and we'll have more on that  decision by uh brandon lewis  uh just a little later let's go back  though now to uh  john craig and back to matt hancock and  uh  well i suppose it was a clear message in  some ways the the virus is back under  control but let's not ruin it  yes he began by talking about that and  right at the end when he was asked  if he had a message for those  conservative mps uh voting against the  government  tomorrow he was saying actually that  some of the tier system is  less restrictive than the national  lockdown but  as you've said right at the beginning he  went through all the reasons why  they had to continue with these  restrictions one point he said  we can afford to let up a little but we  cannot afford to let up a lot  and he said i know we can beat this and  we've got to  stick at it now and he also  talked about um testing and uh following  on from liverpool  300 000 tests there he talked about  going on to test people without  symptoms as well as those who have uh if  you've got uh  um and you if you if you have the uh  virus you can infect others  he called it the silent danger uh then  we came on to questions  um a rather a rather sad question from  uh ella from southampton uh i'm  wondering when she can meet up with her  boyfriend but they don't live together  and they were afraid there was no good  for no good news for ella from  the health secretary there talks about  the rule of six and so on  uh you can't meet inside a household and  then  the second half of that briefing we  heard quite a lot  from someone who i think is a new face  at these briefings  now that's general sir gordon messenger  now he's a former vice chief of the  defense staff  and he's been put in charge of testing  and  he was quite candid in many of his  answers uh perhaps unlike the  politicians doesn't  make such quite bold promises um  he was warning that they can't repeat  the exercise that they've had in  liverpool  where large numbers of soldiers have  been helping out  all over the country that would be  undeliverable he said  um but he did say that that  that obviously that they are going to  try and extend this as much as possible  um then um from from professor stephen  powers we had the warnings  uh that we've seen in this government  document the impact assessment  about the pressure on the nhs in january  and february  where he talked in very monosyllabic  words  talked about the great risks of bugs  trips  uh trips slips and falls and then  finally there was a question about  a warning from nadim zahawi the new  vaccines minister  about how people who didn't or refused  to have a vaccine  might be barred from visiting  hospitality and so on  we didn't really get an answer from mr  hancock on that  but clearly uh what mr zahawi has been  saying is that  perhaps that he's suggesting that  establishments if you haven't got some  sort of certificate or some sort of  proof you've had the vaccine  you might not be allowed entry so that  is something i think we'll hear a lot  more about  but uh quite an optimistic tone from mr  uh  hancock in terms of the numbers that he  mentioned at the beginning  but the message as ever stick at it  can't afford
 

